WEBVTT

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:13.950
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:14.150
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.110
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:43.120
<b>Chapter Eleven                   Colorless Weekend</b>

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.500
Miss, it's time to go.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.750
I know, Suwano.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.310
You haven't come to Saikawa-sensei's office lately.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:03.920
That's because...

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.390
The police told me to avoid contacting him—

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:09.230
Is that all?

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.490
I don't think...

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.420
...he needs me.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.340
Why?

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.040
He admires Dr. Magata.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.730
After what happened,

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.330
I think those feelings are even stronger.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.140
Sensei wants to be free, like she is.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.220
I'm only a chain that holds him 
back from that freedom.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.920
May I share my opinion?

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.140
Yes.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:55.860
I don't know anyone who thinks 
about you as much as he does.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:37.780
Dr. Magata!

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.620
Hello.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.020
How have you been, Saikawa-sensei?

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.360
What are you doing here?

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.610
Did I not promise we would meet again?

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.690
The police are after you.

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:52.330
Shall we go somewhere else?

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.150
Why did you do it?

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.680
The answer is "to go outside."

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.840
That isn't true.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.960
May I have a cigarette?

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.450
Sure.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.620
You smoke?

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:23.320
No, I have never tried it.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.160
You seemed to be enjoying yours so much,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.240
I wanted to try one for myself.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.090
I take no responsibility.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.220
Oh, this...

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.550
It smells like my father.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.020
Are you okay?

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.390
Yes! What an interesting experience!

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.030
Why do you smoke something that's bad for you?

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.560
I don't know.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.890
Perhaps I'm not that attached to life.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.730
No one fears death.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.680
They fear the life that leads to death.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.210
If one could die without suffering, 
no one would fear death, right?

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.290
You're right.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.900
Being alive is, itself, an exception.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:15.340
The natural state of things is death.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:19.250
And life is, itself, a kind of illness.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.560
When the illness is cured, your life vanishes.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.570
You did it to die, didn't you?

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.640
Indeed. My initiation into freedom.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.360
You're going to turn yourself into the police?

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.610
If I do, I might not be executed.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.650
Why don't you...

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:43.650
um, well...

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.670
kill yourself?

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:54.330
I think I would like someone else to kill me.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.500
I want someone else to interfere with my life.

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:02.830
Is that not what wanting to be loved means,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.840
Saikawa-sensei?

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.490
No life is born of its own volition.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.490
Isn't the instinctive desire of beings,

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:17.430
born not of their own volition,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.300
for their lives to be taken in the same way?

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.980
I understand the theory, but...

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:28.950
No, I don't understand it, after all.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:33.930
However, that's simply
 how I'm programmed.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.080
But what you're saying may be right.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.460
It is right for me, but not for you.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:47.070
Either way, the concept of "right" 
means no more than that.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.550
Um, doctor?

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:54.500
Why did you come to see me?

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:58.680
Because I liked what you said in that sea.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.510
"You can't smoke underwater."

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:05.000
A statement I could not have predicted.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:07.530
That is the only reason.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:12.180
You have many eyes.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.700
Miraculously, they do not mix.

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:18.780
No, your other selves created them

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.910
to protect the real you.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.210
Your structure is very similar to my own.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:29.530
What is the difference between you and me?

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:30.900
Hmm...

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:34.220
I would say the biggest difference is our clocks.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:37.070
So, in another hundred years,

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.310
would I be able to be like you?

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.350
One hundred years would not be enough.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:54.570
Someone is waiting for me.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:59.180
Let us say our farewells, Saikawa-sensei.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:02.140
Who is waiting for you?

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.330
The policemen guarding you.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:10.670
I was actually planning to have you kiss me.

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:12.680
But...

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.050
You wouldn't do that.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:17.970
No, I wouldn't.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:20.430
Will you kiss Nishinosono-san?

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.560
Mind your own business, doctor.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.630
The reason she is still alive today

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:31.050
is because of you, yes?

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.800
I don't know.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:37.120
From your perspective, the relationship

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:41.310
between us may have no value at all.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:43.170
What do you mean?

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.050
For example,

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.940
I say a meaningless joke, and she gets irritated.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.310
Instead of giving up, I tell another joke.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:55.990
She gets more irritated.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.210
I've kept that up.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.450
And now, the right amount of time has passed.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.350
That's all.

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:05.950
In other words,

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.860
meaningless acts can sometimes save people?

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.800
Farewell, Saikawa-sensei.

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:51.870
Excuse me.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.070
May I come in, Saikawa-sensei?

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:02.240
Ciao...

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:04.690
What does "ciao" mean?

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:05.750
Goodbye.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.060
Don't say it, then.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:10.880
Did you like that one?

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:13.380
Was that a new joke?

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:14.550
Indeed.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.780
Don't do that, either. It's embarrassing.

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:19.860
I'll make you some coffee.

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:21.510
Cake?

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:22.950
A roll cake.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:27.940
You keep refusing to go with me,
 so I bought it myself.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:32.190
After I have some coffee, I'll leave.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:33.730
I've got this, too.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:36.360
You still have them?

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:38.610
These are the last two.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:42.240
It seems you got those a long time ago.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.080
That's right.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:48.180
It appears I won't need protection anymore.

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.220
So you knew, Sensei...

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.790
Until yesterday,

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:57.480
my uncle said I should stay away from you, too.

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:00.380
That it'd be dangerous if something happened.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:02.240
Terrible, huh?

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.420
Until yesterday?

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.270
They learned that Dr. Magata was in Tokyo.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.970
They caught her accessing a 
computer terminal in Tokyo.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:14.920
So I guess the detectives left 
for Tokyo this morning.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:17.860
Huh? Then what about my guards?

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:19.570
They're gone now.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.470
What's wrong, Sensei?

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:44.620
Oh, nothing.

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:52.100
Dr. Magata pretended to be the 
non-existent Magata Miki,

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:56.330
and appeared on the roof with Director Shindo.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:57.610
Yeah.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:01.090
That's when she stabbed Director Shindo.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:05.360
But Director Shindo accepted his death,

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:07.240
without alerting anyone.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.410
Why was he able to do that?

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.100
Most likely, love.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.250
Being killed is love?

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:19.970
Love has no single shape.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.010
But...

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:24.270
Then, when she died,

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.760
did Dr. Magata's daughter feel love?

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:28.260
I don't know that.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.400
I don't understand!

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.250
I can't believe that's love.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.220
I don't know, either.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:38.530
But for those two, it was probably 
a very natural thing.

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.650
In the end, don't you think the doctor 
just wanted to be free?

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:47.090
And to achieve that, she was willing 
to do whatever it took?

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:50.580
I'm not Dr. Magata...

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:56.790
But don't you know how she feels?

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:01.900
Don't you also want to be free?

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:03.300
Nishinosono-kun?

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:08.760
Because we're the ones who are 
lacking something, right?

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.390
Dr. Magata is more pure than anyone, right?

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:16.270
She's the person you admire, right?

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:20.610
Even if that's true, you have 
no right to blame me for it.

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:22.890
Right now, I'm very sad!

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:24.850
Why won't you understand?!

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.280
I can't help it. We're different people.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:31.480
But...

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:34.190
I'll accept your contradictions.

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:37.710
Even if you start sobbing or hitting me,

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.130
I won't leave this place.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.620
You mustn't cry.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:51.740
If you cry, you'll turn into cheese toast.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:54.880
That's right, isn't it?

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:57.500
Nishinosono-kun?

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:58.850
Yes?

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:03.780
The first time I took one of 
Nishinosono-sensei's classes...

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:05.740
You mean my dad?

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:08.990
In the very first lesson, he asked this:

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:13.080
"Did you all do your reviews?"

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:17.250
And I answered honestly and said, "No."

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:19.050
And he said,

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.580
"Okay, study the first chapter for next week."

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.490
That was all he said. And then he left the classroom.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.040
The next week,

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.180
I'd mostly reviewed it.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:37.730
When class started, he said this:

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.060
"Was there anything you didn't understand?"

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:44.320
No one answered.

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:49.130
"If you understand, there's nothing 
left for me to do.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:52.630
Read chapter two for next week," he said.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.190
And he left the classroom again.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:56.830
A pretty easy job, huh?

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:58.530
And the next week,

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:03.090
I read the book seriously and 
thought up some questions.

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:06.090
And I asked my question.

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:07.590
What do you think happened?

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.340
He said, "Figure it out for yourself," didn't he?

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.110
No, he spent the next four weeks

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:16.080
answering my question.

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.650
And at the end, here's what he said:

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:27.230
"This is my answer to Saikawa Sohei-kun's question.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:28.790
What's the next question?

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:33.450
Does anyone else have questions 
from chapters one and two?"

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:36.370
Sorry.

00:15:37.330 --> 00:15:39.710
That wasn't a very interesting story, huh?

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:42.150
No...

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:44.780
Just hearing about my dad

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:46.380
makes me happy.

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:51.320
Lately, there aren't any teachers like him.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:56.760
Several years later, I met you.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:58.020
The magic trick!

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:01.200
Yeah, you were so proud of it.

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:05.270
Because no one had ever figured it out before.

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:08.280
At that moment,

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:12.010
I hated you.

00:16:13.130 --> 00:16:15.380
How many years has it been?

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:18.130
Nearly ten.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:19.260
I see.

00:16:20.030 --> 00:16:23.880
The girl who almost cried when 
I figured out her magic trick

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:26.060
grew up into

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:29.130
a girl who looked good in a purple dress.

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:33.880
I liked that dress.

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:35.800
I knew that.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:38.620
That's why I wore it that day.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:42.590
After that day, you changed a little.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:44.260
Yes.

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.910
Your makeup and clothes became flashier.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.650
That's right.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:53.780
And the way you expressed your feelings 
became more and more extreme.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:56.630
Yes, I won't deny that.

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:59.540
I was a little worried.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:00.890
Not just a little...

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:03.790
You were always by my side.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:07.420
Thanks to you, I learned many more jokes.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:08.670
Too many.

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:10.590
I'll start narrowing them down.

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:14.300
No, please keep at it.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:16.300
Thanks.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:23.180
Sensei?

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:24.180
What?

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:28.070
Did you ever tell Gido-san stuff like this?

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:29.270
Setsuko?

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:32.940
When you're alone, do you ever talk 
about important things like this?

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:35.700
No... Her husband's usually with us.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:38.700
She's married?

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.200
Her name changed to "Gido" after she married.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:44.460
Um... Are you and she...

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:45.830
She's my little sister.

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.780
She's a bit weird, but she's an interesting girl.

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:52.460
No way. Then that T-shirt...

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:54.970
Yeah... Setsuko gave it to me.

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:54.970
<b>Eggplant</b>

00:17:57.270 --> 00:18:00.690
Sensei! From now on, I'll buy your shirts!

00:18:00.690 --> 00:18:02.110
Understood?

00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:03.180
Go ahead.

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:06.060
Oh.

00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:09.790
I kept meaning to say this.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:10.970
What?

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:13.120
That dried shiitake mushroom...

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:13.980
Yes?

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.390
It's good for anemia,

00:18:17.390 --> 00:18:19.160
but you can't eat it like that.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:22.210
You soak it in water.

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:26.100
And then, you can eat it or use it for soup stock.

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:30.670
Why didn't you tell me that until now?

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:33.580
I was waiting for you to notice.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:36.350
Did you deliberately not tell me?

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.220
I couldn't say.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:39.680
Jeez!

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:42.930
Mother...

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:48.190
I think humans need the sea more than the Moon.

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:52.890
That means that it is strongly 
connected to humanity.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:55.840
Humans are connected to many

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:57.320
other things, aren't they?

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:58.900
Indeed.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:02.090
Every individual human affects

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:04.690
the surrounding environment.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:10.710
But humans do not exist for the sake of 
the things and people around them.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:16.320
Often, they mistakenly think 
that they wish to exist

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:20.340
for the sake of others, or to help everyone.

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:24.610
They cannot accept that they don't 
know why they exist, yes?

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:27.980
They always want an answer.

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:31.480
That is the nature of the animals called "humans."

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:33.790
Is it wrong to want an answer?

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:35.230
No.

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:38.450
It is not wrong to want one.

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:42.190
But there is no perfect answer.

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:47.390
But continuing to seek one is very important.

00:19:47.390 --> 00:19:49.770
So you can get close to it, right?

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:52.880
You could think of it that way.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.930
But I do not yet know.

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:59.510
Then there are things even you don't understand?

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:00.840
Of course.

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:05.510
Humans can be kind because there 
are things they do not understand.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:07.360
Why?

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.210
If they knew everything,

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:12.730
they would attempt nothing.

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:15.340
If they attempted nothing,

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:17.780
nothing new would happen.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:23.590
Humans seek answers to the 
things they do not know.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:27.280
That is how kindness,

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:31.880
nostalgia, joy, and fun are born.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:35.920
I always ask you questions.

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:38.830
By seeking answers this way,

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:41.450
will I be able to become kind?

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:43.800
Yes.

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:46.880
Always continue seeking, even when I am gone.

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:51.060
Continue seeking, even when no one answers.

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.830
Seek the answers within yourself.

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.540
You must not forget that.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:00.480
And that will become your kindness.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:04.820
I understand, Mother.

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:11.120
Michiru? This is Kishio.

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:12.890
Say hello.

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.120
Oh?

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:17.650
You're here, as well, Uncle?

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:20.700
Yes, this is Michiru.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.090
She's grown up, has she not?
